    \u0432 \u043c\u0430\u0441\u0441\u043e\u0432\u044b\u0445 \u0440\u043e\u0437\u044b\u0433\u0440\u044b\u0448\u0430\u0445."
  interestingFactsEn: "Gothlob Fregue's life is full of not only scientific breakthroughs, but also amazing, paradoxical and even tragic stories that make his personality even more multifaceted. These are the most interesting and interesting facts about him.\n\nThe figure for the experts: Recognition, which came too late, is one of the most bitter ironic stories in the history of science. Today, Fregue is one of the pillars of modern logic, but in life he was almost unknown outside a narrow circle of scientists. His great work \"Checking of concepts\" (1879) was met with great restraint, and many contemporarys simply did not understand his innovation.His \"crusal father\" became Bertrand Russell, who in 1903 annexed the concept of Fregue to his \"Fundaments of Mathematics\" to the public, making it accessible to the public. The real glory came to Frega only after World War II, when many of the logic and philosophers who emigrated to the United States (such as Rudolf Carnap, Kurt Godel and Alfred Tarsky) popularized his ideas and translated his works into English.Fregue himself, sending his unpublished manuscripts to his adopted son, as if he had anticipated his postmortem glory, writing, \"I believe there are things here that will one day be of much greater value than now. Make sure nothing is lost.\" These papers were, alas, destroyed during the bombing of Münster in 1945.\n\n♪ An analyst in the department: Lecture plays with a single actor of the Students' Memories paint the image of Frege as a highly isolated and indiscreet man, and his lectures were essentially monospectives: he stood to the audience with his back, wrote on the board the elaborate symbols of his two-dimensional logic and explained them without looking at the audience. Rudolph Carnap, later famous philosopher, recalled: \"Frege looked older than he was.He was small, rather shy, extremely introverted. He rarely looked at the audience. We usually only saw his back.\" No student ever asked questions -- either during the lecture or after. The possibility of discussion seemed impossible. Only in one of the advanced courses, where there were only three listeners, he was a little bit tanning, but the questions weren't asked.\n\nThe political diary and the shadow of anti-Semitism, Fregue's encampment, had a darker side, and he was known to have been a member of the far-right \"Vatlandspartei\" and anti-Semite, and his \"political diary\" written one year before his death (1924) was full of chauvinistic records, where he complained, among other things, of \"the Jewish community\" and their civil rights.This knowledge, according to one of his students (known philosopher Gershom Scholem), explains why even non-Jewish students were reluctant to approach a professor outside the university, his reputation being generally known.\n\nThe letter that shocked Fregue may have been the most dramatic episode in Fregue's biography in 1902, received a letter from young Bertrand Russell, when his second volume of main work, \"Basic Laws of arithmetic\", was already in print. Russell set out his famous paradox (on the multitude of all the layers that do not contain themselves), which destroyed the logical system of Fregue, built on the basis of classrooms.In his reply, he wrote: \"Your discovery of the contradiction surprised me beyond the measure and, I would say, almost stunned, because it shaken the soil on which I wanted to build arithmetic. ...Arifmetics is waning!\" He bravely added to the end of the volume a hasty app in which he acknowledged the error.Russell himself later admired this act as an act of the highest intellectual honesty: \"When he discovered that his basic assumption was wrong, he responded with intellectual pleasure, clearly suppressing any sense of frustration. It was almost superhuman.\"\n\nThe irony: \"Logica\" or \"Silach Gothlib\"? We'll finish it with chicken. Searching for Gothlob Fregue sometimes leads to unexpected results. For example, a German folk story called \"Silach Gothlib\", which in the test turns out to be completely unrelated to our hero. And one of the papers mentions how someone who signed the name Gothlob Fregue put out leaflets of the city with threats on behalf of... iguana.This is a vivid example of how the name of a great but little-known philosopher, in time, has found his own, sometimes absurd, life in pop culture and even in mass pranks."
